  "configuration_id": "config_033",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_032 (multimodal, multiprofile, scvi_n200, resnet50, transformer, concat) is the best result so far at 0.688 acc / 0.659 F1, outperforming the RNA-only multiprofile baseline (config_006, 0.653) by ~3.5 points. However, we have only tested concat fusion in the multimodal setting; the weighted fusion alternative is completely unexplored. Question: Does the fusion strategy (concat vs. weighted) materially affect multimodal performance, or is the 3.5-point image gain robust to how modalities are combined? Why this experiment: config_033 changes exactly one variable (fusion: concat → weighted) relative to config_032, making it a clean A/B test that isolates the fusion mechanism. If weighted fusion improves performance, it reveals that modality importance is asymmetric and we should invest in tuning weights; if performance is similar, we can rule out fusion strategy as a bottleneck and redirect budget toward improving the image encoder (e.g., ViT-L) or exploring other axes. Under a limited budget, this single-variable comparison maximizes information per experiment by directly extending our strongest result while resolving a fundamental design uncertainty that affects all future multimodal configurations."
}

